 4. Executives are likelier to trust AI&#x27;s judgment 
 
 By Emily Peck 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Executives are twice as likely as rank-and-file workers to trust AI over their own judgment, per a survey out this morning.

 Why it matters: It's a worrying development. Leaders are hired for their experience and knowledge. 

 Outsourcing that to AI raises the risk of making decisions that don't line up with reality, which then reverberate across companies and beyond. By the numbers: 32.8% of executives said that they trust AI more than their own judgment, according to the survey from Charter, a workplace research and media company.

 That's twice the rate for workers, or individual contributors. Just 16.3% of individual contributors said the same. The divide shows up at the level below executives — 34.8% of managers said they trust AI more than their own judgment. Zoom out: "Cognitive offloading" is a term for using tools like AI to reduce mental effort. 

 "Executives — despite having the most experience — are doing it more than anyone," the report's co-authors wrote. The fine print: Charter in August surveyed 5,002 U.S. office workers, at companies with at least 100 employees, who said they use AI at work, including 2,952 individual contributors, 1,547 managers and 503 executives — including vice presidents, C-suite executives, owners and founders.

 
 
